---
import { getEntry } from "astro:content";
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";

const entry = await getEntry("home", "page");
if (!entry) {
  throw new Error("Missing home page content");
}

const email = entry.data.footer.email;
const meta = {
  title: "Security at Marres | Marres",
  description:
    "Read the verified application and hosting controls that protect Marres accounts, tenant data, provider calls, and secrets.",
  canonicalUrl: "https://marres.io/security",
};
---

<SiteLayout {...meta}>
  <article class="legal-page">
    <div class="legal-content">
      <span class="tag">Trust</span>
      <h1>Security</h1>
      <p class="legal-date">Updated 3 September 2026</p>
      <p class="legal-intro">
        This page lists controls verified in the current Marres application and
        required before production launch. Marres does not claim a
        certification, independent audit, or security guarantee.
      </p>

      <section>
        <h2>Accounts and passwords</h2>
        <p>
          Marres hashes passwords with Argon2id and a unique random salt. Plain
          passwords are not stored. Password work runs in a bounded blocking
          task pool so login traffic cannot create an unbounded set of password
          jobs.
        </p>
        <p>
          Unknown accounts receive dummy password-hash work, and login errors do
          not reveal whether an email address exists.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Sessions and request protection</h2>
        <p>
          Sessions are stored on the server in PostgreSQL. Session cookies are
          HTTP-only and use SameSite=Lax. Public HTTPS deployment must enable
          the Secure cookie flag. Login rotates the session identifier, and
          logout invalidates the server-side session.
        </p>
        <p>
          Browser form submissions for authentication, onboarding, billing, and
          logout require a session-bound CSRF token. Marres compares submitted
          tokens in constant time and rejects missing or invalid tokens.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Tenant access</h2>
        <p>
          Each user resolves through one tenant membership. Protected analysis,
          onboarding, and billing queries include the resolved tenant.
          Owner-only actions check the membership role again before changing
          billing or onboarding data.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Provider boundaries and provenance</h2>
        <p>
          Apify collection uses a pinned Actor build with limits on reviews,
          time, response size, concurrency, and charge. It is configured not to
          collect reviewer personal data.
        </p>
        <p>
          OpenRouter requests pin Luna and embedding models to OpenAI, disable
          provider fallback, and apply limits on time, response size,
          concurrency, retries, and cost. Marres records provider, model, build,
          attempt, input, output, and source provenance needed to inspect a run.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Hosting and secrets</h2>
        <p>
          Before public launch, the production configuration requires HTTPS,
          permits public traffic only on port 443, terminates TLS at a reverse
          proxy, keeps the app on a local interface, and requires a TLS-enabled
          PostgreSQL connection. The app.marres.io cutover is not complete.
        </p>
        <p>
          The application reads its database, Stripe, Apify, and OpenRouter
          credentials at runtime instead of storing them in source code. The
          production configuration covers database, DNS, and repository
          credentials in encrypted configuration and restricted runtime files.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Report a concern</h2>
        <p>
          Email <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a> with a security concern. Include
          the affected page, what you observed, and how we can contact you. Do not
          send passwords, payment details, private keys, or other secrets by email.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</SiteLayout>
